@ilyavolodin No, it's a scoping thing. He can't create the function outside the loop because it closes over a loop variable.

``` js
for (var a = [], i = 0; i < 5; ++i) {
  a.push(function(){ return i; });
}
a.map(function(f){ return f(); }); // [5, 5, 5, 5, 5]
```

But this reminds me that @julienw is using const, which is let-scooped, so wouldn't have the same problem as the var in my example. That should be made an exception.
